1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,321
How do we know a new merchant really owns the email they typed?

2
00:00:03,321 --> 00:00:07,833
Anyone can type any address, so a sign-up alone proves nothing.

3
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:13,428
So the partner module saves the merchant as pending, and sends a one-time code to that email.

4
00:00:13,428 --> 00:00:18,547
The code lives in Redis with a configured lifetime, and disappears when it expires.

5
00:00:18,547 --> 00:00:24,202
The email goes out through the email port, and locally it lands in Mailpit, where we can open it.

6
00:00:24,202 --> 00:00:29,369
The merchant types the code back, and only then is the email marked as verified.

7
00:00:29,369 --> 00:00:33,857
Wrong codes count against a configured limit, and too many lock the attempt.

8
00:00:33,857 --> 00:00:37,452
Resends are limited too, so nobody can flood an inbox.

9
00:00:37,452 --> 00:00:41,857
Every failure returns a clear error with a code, never a stack trace.

10
00:00:41,857 --> 00:00:46,523
Next, the merchant moves through a lifecycle, from pending to active.

